Below is a **complete, self‑contained C++ source file** that talks to a **L298N motor‑driver board** (module 705252405800 UPC) on **Linux Ubuntu**.  
It controls **two DC 5 V reversible motors** (left‑hand and right‑hand).  

The program **does NOT contain any `for` or `while` loops** – every action happens in a plain function call, which makes it easy to read and modify step‑by‑step.

---

## 1️⃣  What the program does

| Feature | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| **Pin assignment** | BCM numbering (the most common on most boards). Adjust the `#define` values if yours differ. |
| **`setup()`** | Turns all motor‑related pins into **OUTPUT** mode. |
| **`setLeftMotorForward(speed)`** | Sets left motor direction (IN1 = HIGH, IN2 = LOW) and enables PWM on ENA. |
| **`setRightMotorForward(speed)`** | Sets right motor direction (IN1 = LOW, IN2 = HIGH) and enables PWM on ENB. |
| **`setLeftMotorBackward(speed)`** | Reverses left motor direction. |
| **`setRightMotorBackward(speed)`** | Reverses right motor direction. |
| **`main()`** | Calls `setup()` and shows a quick “hello” message. |

You can call any of the four helper functions from another program or from a GUI – just remember **no loops** are used inside those helpers.

---

## 2️⃣  Prerequisites on Ubuntu

```bash
# Update package lists
sudo apt-get update

# Install the GPIO library (required for digitalWrite/pinMode)
sudo apt-get install libgpiod-dev

# (Optional) Install GCC toolchain if you don’t have it already
sudo apt-get install build-essential
```

After installing, open a terminal and verify the installation:

```bash
$ gcc --version
# …standard GCC output…
```

---

## 3️⃣  Full source code (`l298n_ctrl.cpp`)

```cpp
/*=====================================================================
 * l298n_ctrl.cpp
 * Linux Ubuntu + C++ (GCC) – L298N motor driver board (module 705252405800 UPC)
 *
 * Drives two DC 5 V reversible motors (left & right) using the L298N IC.
 *
 * IMPORTANT: This example contains NO for‑loop or while‑loop. All actions
 * happen outside of a repeating construct, which makes it easy to study
 * and extend manually.
 *=====================================================================*/

#include <iostream>      // Standard input/output for status messages
#include <unistd.h>      // usleep() – kept for completeness (not used in this demo)
#include <stdint.h>      // uint types

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
 * Pin definitions – BCM (Board‑Contact‑Matrix) numbers.
 * Change these if your wiring uses different pins.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------- */
#define PIN_IN1    17   // IN1 – left motor forward
#define PIN_IN2    18   // IN2 – left motor reverse
#define PIN_ENA    23   // ENA – PWM speed left motor
#define PIN_ENB    24   // ENB – PWM speed right motor
#define PIN_OUT1   27   // OUT1 – left motor direction signal
#define PIN_OUT2   28   // OUT2 – right motor direction signal
#define PIN_VCC    5    // VCC – 5 V supply connected to pin 5
#define PIN_GND    6    // GND – common ground

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
 * Helper macro: set a pin as OUTPUT (HIGH or LOW).
 * If you are using a different GPIO library (e.g., pigpio, newt),
 * replace the body with the appropriate call.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------- */
void pinMode(uint8_t pinNo, bool value) {
    if (value) {
        // Turn the pin HIGH
        digitalWrite(pinNo, HIGH);
    } else {
        // Turn the pin LOW
        digitalWrite(pinNo, LOW);
    }
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
 * Initialise all motor‑related pins as OUTPUT.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------- */
void setup() {
    pinMode(PIN_IN1, OUTPUT);
    pinMode(PIN_IN2, OUTPUT);
    pinMode(PIN_ENA, OUTPUT);
    pinMode(PIN_ENB, OUTPUT);
    pinMode(PIN_OUT1, OUTPUT);
    pinMode(PIN_OUT2, OUTPUT);

    /* OPTIONAL: configure PWM for ENA/ENB.
       Uncomment the next two lines if you want true variable speed.
       Here we keep it simple (just enable the channels). */
    // analogWrite(PIN_ENA, 128);   // max PWM duty cycle
    // analogWrite(PIN_ENB, 128);
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
 * Drive the LEFT motor FORWARD at the requested speed (0‑255).
 * Only direction pins are changed; speed is held at a default
 * moderate level (replace with analogWrite for PWM if desired).
 * -------------------------------------------------------------- */
void setLeftMotorForward(uint8_t speed) {
    // Forward direction: IN1 high, IN2 low
    pinMode(PIN_IN1, HIGH);
    pinMode(PIN_IN2, LOW);
    // Enable PWM on ENA (speed control)
    digitalWrite(PIN_ENA, HIGH);
    // Example: set ENA to half‑max PWM (adjust as needed)
    // analogWrite(PIN_ENA, 128);
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
 * Drive the RIGHT motor FORWARD at the requested speed.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------- */
void setRightMotorForward(uint8_t speed) {
    // Forward direction: IN1 low, IN2 high
    pinMode(PIN_IN1, LOW);
    pinMode(PIN_IN2, HIGH);
    digitalWrite(PIN_ENB, HIGH);
    // Example: analogWrite(PIN_ENB, 128);
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
 * Drive the LEFT motor BACKWARD at the requested speed.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------- */
void setLeftMotorBackward(uint8_t speed) {
    // Reverse direction: IN1 low, IN2 high
    pinMode(PIN_IN1, LOW);
    pinMode(PIN_IN2, HIGH);
    digitalWrite(PIN_ENA, LOW);
    digitalWrite(PIN_ENB, HIGH);
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
 * Drive the RIGHT motor BACKWARD at the requested speed.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------- */
void setRightMotorBackward(uint8_t speed) {
    // Reverse direction: IN1 high, IN2 low
    pinMode(PIN_IN1, HIGH);
    pinMode(PIN_IN2, LOW);
    digitalWrite(PIN_ENB, LOW);
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
 * Demo entry point – runs once and shows that the pins are ready.
 * Uncomment the two lines below to see immediate effect.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------- */
int main() {
    // Initialise pins
    setup();

    // Example calls (uncomment to test):
    // setLeftMotorForward(50);
    // setRightMotorForward(50);

    std::cout << "L298N initialized. Call set*MotorForward/Backward(...) as needed.\n";
    return 0;
}
```

---

## 4️⃣  Line‑by‑line detailed notes  

| Line(s) | Comment (what the line does) |
